1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,759
It is hard to believe that New York City was once a small Dutch settlement.

2
00:00:07,759 --> 00:00:14,120
An old document shows that Dutch settlers did in fact pay for this land.

3
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:19,679
The document can be seen at the city's South Street Seaport Museum.

4
00:00:19,679 --> 00:00:24,560
Martin Berenser is head of the Netherlands National Archives.

5
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:29,960
He says the letter tells about a ship arriving in Amsterdam.

6
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:37,640
The ship's operator said the settlement is going well and we bought the island of Manhattan

7
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:40,039
for 60 guilders.

8
00:00:40,039 --> 00:00:45,039
The letter is on loan for New York's 400th anniversary.

9
00:00:45,039 --> 00:00:50,920
Berenser says the letter tells about the settlement which became New York.

10
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:54,799
The museum also has a map of the settlement.

11
00:00:54,799 --> 00:00:59,520
Another map shows just how small Manhattan was.

12
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:03,400
It changed when Britain took control.

13
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:10,879
Historian Barry Lewis says the British filled in part of the East River with waste because

14
00:01:10,879 --> 00:01:13,040
more land was needed.

15
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:18,000
We had only about 100,000 people at the beginning of the 19th century.

16
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,400
By 1875 the population of Manhattan is 1 million people.

17
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:27,800
By 1910 over 2 million people are living in Manhattan Island.

18
00:01:27,799 --> 00:01:34,920
The Brooklyn Bridge was built in 1883 so people could move to Long Island where land

19
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:37,479
was less costly.

20
00:01:37,479 --> 00:01:45,400
Lewis says people crossed the East River by carriage or boat or they walked across the bridge.

21
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:53,039
He says New York's underground trains led to the development of land in areas far from

22
00:01:53,039 --> 00:01:55,560
the center of the city.

23
00:01:55,560 --> 00:02:02,960
Many businesses needed to be in Manhattan so developers started building up creating the

24
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:04,600
skyscraper.

25
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:10,159
People were afraid it would fall down in the first wind storm and anyone who had property

26
00:02:10,159 --> 00:02:15,719
in the commercial buildings next to it, they were terrified that nobody would rent in

27
00:02:15,719 --> 00:02:18,960
their buildings because that thing was next door.

28
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:22,280
But more and more skyscrapers were built.

29
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:25,319
Each one was higher than the next.

30
00:02:25,319 --> 00:02:30,759
Lewis says the big reason for so many skyscrapers was money.

31
00:02:30,759 --> 00:02:36,079
He says New York was built by investments in land.

32
00:02:36,079 --> 00:02:39,159
That is not why everything was built.

33
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:42,719
Some structures have improved the look of the city.

34
00:02:42,719 --> 00:02:48,639
Two examples are the Washington Square Arch and the Guggenheim Museum.

35
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:56,080
But money is still important on the island that began as a trading center four centuries

36
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:57,080
ago.

37
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:03,520
The area of the Dutch settled is now Wall Street, the world's financial capital.

38
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:04,760
I'm Steve Ember.

